WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.061 --> 00:00:03.640
- The market for cultural art is huge.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.208
Unfortunately, almost all the native art

00:00:05.208 --> 00:00:07.781
that you find either online or in stores

00:00:07.781 --> 00:00:10.217
features fake Native American art.

00:00:10.217 --> 00:00:13.758
When I walk into a store
and I see cultural art

00:00:13.758 --> 00:00:16.197
from my community that's counterfeit,

00:00:16.197 --> 00:00:20.354
it feels like a family photo
on a product in a store.

00:00:20.354 --> 00:00:22.055
It feels that personal to me,

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:23.603
and I wanted to do something about it.

00:00:23.603 --> 00:00:26.770
(inspirational music)

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:36.645
My name's Louie Gong.

00:00:36.645 --> 00:00:37.998
I'm a NookSack tribal member,

00:00:37.998 --> 00:00:39.720
and I'm the founder of Eighth Generation,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.452
which is based at Pike
Place Market in Seattle.

00:00:44.452 --> 00:00:45.672
When I walk through the mall,

00:00:45.672 --> 00:00:48.918
almost every store has
native art on products in it,

00:00:48.918 --> 00:00:50.485
and not one of those products

00:00:50.485 --> 00:00:52.943
is connected to a native artist.

00:00:52.943 --> 00:00:55.374
To me, that represents a huge problem,

00:00:55.374 --> 00:00:58.520
and Eighth Generation tries
to address that problem

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.515
by creating actual opportunities

00:01:00.515 --> 00:01:03.932
for cultural artists to develop products.

00:01:04.829 --> 00:01:07.679
I'm a self-taught artist,
and about 10 years ago

00:01:07.679 --> 00:01:11.169
I started doing art by
customizing a pair of shoes,

00:01:11.169 --> 00:01:13.946
and I had developed a really
strong, organic following

00:01:13.946 --> 00:01:15.579
from doing that work.

00:01:15.579 --> 00:01:17.215
We know that people
want to but Native art,

00:01:17.215 --> 00:01:19.305
so that eventually became the foundation

00:01:19.305 --> 00:01:20.972
of Eighth Generation.

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:25.581
We have about 15 different
business relationships

00:01:25.581 --> 00:01:29.168
with makers within two
miles of Pike Place Market.

00:01:29.168 --> 00:01:32.405
When you buy products featuring
art done by native people

00:01:32.405 --> 00:01:34.003
and produced by native-owned companies,

00:01:34.003 --> 00:01:36.066
you're strengthening the cultural art

00:01:36.066 --> 00:01:40.163
and the communities
whose artwork you like.

00:01:40.163 --> 00:01:41.248
We're the first native-owned company

00:01:41.248 --> 00:01:42.886
to offer wool blankets.

00:01:42.886 --> 00:01:45.318
This is important, because
the aesthetic of wool blankets

00:01:45.318 --> 00:01:46.999
has largely been native.

00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:48.168
When people think of wool blankets,

00:01:48.168 --> 00:01:49.963
they think of native art.

00:01:49.963 --> 00:01:52.849
I think people are used
to seeing native people

00:01:52.849 --> 00:01:54.560
in one of three categories.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.601
We're typically a charity
project, or a symbol of history,

00:01:58.601 --> 00:02:00.676
or a symbol of the natural environment.

00:02:00.676 --> 00:02:04.080
We're planting a seed about
who native people really are,

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.230
and so when they walk in the door,

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:06.943
the message that gets conveyed

00:02:06.943 --> 00:02:09.794
is that we're alive and we're thriving.

00:02:09.794 --> 00:02:13.215
And that contradicts
what people want to think

00:02:13.215 --> 00:02:14.882
about native people.

